---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import PostFigure from '../../components/PostFigure.astro';
import ImageStrip from '../../components/ImageStrip.astro';

import journeyMap2 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-2.png';
import raaJourney from '../../assets/images/raa-journey.png';
import raaJourneyDirectory from '../../assets/images/raa-journey-directory.png';
import designSystem2 from '../../assets/images/design-system-2.png';
import dsButtons from '../../assets/images/raa-ds-buttons.png';
import appAlerts from '../../assets/images/raa-app-ds-alerts.png';
import uofaStyleguide from '../../assets/images/uofa-styleguide.png';
---

<PostLayout slug="making-the-experience-visible">
	<p>
		Most organisations know more about their customers than they can use. Analytics sit in one tool, research reports
		in another, contact centre data somewhere else, and the knowledge of how a screen should behave lives in a few
		designers' heads. When that knowledge is hard to find, teams guess, repeat work, or wait for the one person who
		knows.
	</p>
	<p>
		I've come to see journey mapping and design systems as two answers to the same problem. Both make knowledge
		visible so that people can help themselves.
	</p>

	<h2>A journey map is only useful if it stays alive</h2>
	<p>
		The value of a journey map isn't the map. It's what happens when it stays connected to real data and real
		decisions. A map made for one project, printed once and pinned to a wall, is out of date within months.
	</p>
	<p>
		A living journey sits in a journey management platform. Each stage is connected to analytics such as drop-off and
		completion rates, to research findings tagged to the step where they happened, and to benchmarking results over
		time. Solutions are tagged to the stage they fix and prioritised by customer value and business value, so anyone
		can see what's on the roadmap, in delivery, or on hold.
	</p>
	<PostFigure
		src={journeyMap2}
		alt="Journey explorer with a solution panel open, listing its priority, effort, customer value and business value"
		caption="A journey explorer with a solution open, showing priority, effort, customer value and business value."
	/>
	<p>
		When a product team arrives at an ideation session with that shared view, the time goes into the real unknowns
		instead of re-establishing what's already known.
	</p>

	<h2>Built from evidence, not assumption</h2>
	<p>
		Before we had digital journey tools, I built maps by hand from research synthesis: interviews, usability sessions,
		analytics and team knowledge. I worked alongside service designers, who built the service blueprints while I
		mapped the digital products. The most useful thing those maps showed wasn't the pain points. It was the gaps: the
		stages where the team was making decisions with no research at all. Those gaps became the next priorities for
		discovery.
	</p>
	<PostFigure
		src={raaJourney}
		alt="Journey map with stages from research to booking, and rows for moods, touchpoints, pain points and opportunities"
		caption="A journey map from research, showing stages from research to booking with moods, touchpoints, pain points and opportunities."
	/>
	<p>
		We kept every map in one shared directory across product areas, tagged by lifecycle stage and opportunity type, so
		teams could see where the experience had been mapped and where it hadn't.
	</p>
	<PostFigure
		src={raaJourneyDirectory}
		alt="Miro journey map directory showing road service, car insurance and home insurance product areas"
		caption="A shared directory of journey maps across road service, car insurance and home insurance."
	/>

	<h2>A design system is the same idea, for the interface</h2>
	<p>
		A design system makes the knowledge of how the interface should look and behave visible: components, patterns,
		design tokens, accessibility notes and coded examples, all in one place. When it's documented and governed well,
		product and engineering teams can build standard patterns without waiting on a designer. Designers write lighter
		specs, and the time saved goes back into discovery and testing.
	</p>
	<p>
		At RAA the team built the design system in-house in Figma, with design tokens and documentation published for
		engineers, covering the website, insurance, membership, travel, payments and the native app. Through a partnership
		with Vision Australia, accessibility standards were built in at the component level, so they were designed in from
		the start rather than audited after delivery.
	</p>
	<ImageStrip
		label="Design system examples"
		items={[
			{
				src: designSystem2,
				alt: 'Component documentation page showing anatomy, usage guidelines and specs',
				caption: 'Component documentation with anatomy, usage and specs',
			},
			{
				src: dsButtons,
				alt: 'Button documentation showing anatomy, spacing, typography, states and accessibility guidelines',
				caption: 'Buttons, with anatomy, states and accessibility notes',
			},
			{
				src: appAlerts,
				alt: 'App design system alerts in information, confirmation, warning, error and tip variants',
				caption: 'Alert variants for the native app',
			},
		]}
	/>
	<p>
		Earlier, at the University of Adelaide, I was on the website rebuild from the start and wrote the front-end code.
		That code became a custom JavaScript design system, with every component documented in a style guide, so every
		delivery team built to the same standard.
	</p>
	<PostFigure
		src={uofaStyleguide}
		alt="University of Adelaide style guide page documenting the audio player and playlist components, with HTML code examples"
		caption="The University of Adelaide style guide, with documented components and code examples."
	/>

	<h2>Connecting the two</h2>
	<p>
		The real gain comes when these sources are connected. I built an AI-assisted workflow that linked the design
		system, journey maps and research synthesis, so a designer could ask a question and get an answer drawn from all of
		them. A small team can keep its quality high when what it knows is easy to find.
	</p>
	<p>
		My view is simple: if knowledge only lives in people's heads, it leaves when they do. Making the experience
		visible, in a living journey and a well-kept design system, is how a team keeps getting better rather than starting
		again each time.
	</p>
</PostLayout>
